```java
class Account {
    private int balance;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    void transferTo(Account to, int amount) {
        synchronized (this.lock) {
            synchronized (to.lock) {
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
}

// Thread 1: a.transferTo(b, 10);
// Thread 2: b.transferTo(a, 20);
```

Thread 1 locks `a` then waits for `b`. Thread 2 locks `b` then waits for `a` — the exact two-lock cycle from s04, hidden inside symmetric-looking method calls. Because `transferTo` always locks `this` first, the order two threads acquire locks in depends entirely on which account each call started from.

```java
class Account {
    private int balance;
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    private final int id;

    void transferTo(Account to, int amount) {
        Account first = this.id < to.id ? this : to;
        Account second = this.id < to.id ? to : this;
        synchronized (first.lock) {
            synchronized (second.lock) {
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Every thread now locks accounts in the same order — lower ID first — regardless of which account's `transferTo` it called. There's no longer a way for two threads to each hold one lock while waiting on the other, which breaks circular wait and removes the cycle entirely.
